(savannah), and urban. The jungle (sylatic) transmission cycle involves transmission of the virus between nonhuman primates and mosquito species found in the forest canopy. The virus is transmitted via mosquitoes from nonhuman primates to human when the humans encroach into the jungle during occupational or recreational activities. The urban transmission cycle involves transmission of the virus between human and urban mosquitoes, primarily Aedes aegypti. Viremic humans traveling from one region to another can feed into and serve as a source of infection for mosquitoes in other transmission cycles. In Africa, an intermediate (savannah) cycle involves transmission of YFV from tree-hole-breeding Aedes spp. to humans living or working in jungle border areas. In this cycle, the virus can be transmitted from nonhuman primate to humans or from human to human via these mosquitoes.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":834,"renderedHeight":489,"naturalWidth":834,"naturalHeight":489,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 979 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":20904},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/r5907a1f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows the areas of Africa and South America in which the World Health Organization has indicated that yellow fever has been reported currently or in the past and areas where vectors and animal reservoirs currently exist.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":834,"renderedHeight":367,"naturalWidth":834,"naturalHeight":367,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":44054},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":f